  "query_preview": "..\n\nA strong upper level ridge (600 DAM at 500 MB) will be retrograding \nwest through Tennessee over the next day. Deep pseudo-tropical \nmoisture (12z DTX sounding indicated a very impressive 7 C dew pt at \n700 MB) will remain nearly stationary over far southern Lower \nMichigan and the Ohio River Valley. It will not take much forcing to \ninitiate showers, and looks like a series of subtle shortwaves will \ncontinue to track out of the Midwest and track along/close to the \nsouthern Michigan border through Thursday morning. The forecast is \nskewed toward higher pops, as EURO ensembles indicate a 24 hr qpf of \na tenth of an inch or more along and south of M-59 in the tonight-\nThursday timeframe. Instability looks modest into southern Lower \nMichigan with weak mid level lapse rates, while more significant \nCAPE holds south of border. Thus, any thunderstorms that develop are \nnot expected to be severe, but will serve to enhance rainfall. \n\nAs the upper level ridge continues to track westward, the ridge axis \nwill extend into the Northern Plains, allowing upper level flow to \nbecome west-northwest over the central Great Lakes later Thursday. \nThis will support subsidence and a drying-out process as a \npronounced surface ridge, extending from strong high over northern \nOntario, moves in for Thursday night and Friday. \n\nHowever, showers will return in earnest for the Weekend as low \npressure comes out of the Central Plains and deep pseudo tropical \nmoisture returns. Euro ensembles indicate > 70% chance of half an \ninch or more or rainfall, with 40-60% chance of one inch or more in \nthe Saturday into Saturday night timeframe. Uncertainty if there \nwill be another substantial round of showers on Sunday or Monday, as \none last trailing upper level wave moves through, shearing out in \nthe process.",
